data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"researcher","content":"- CRISPR gene editing modifies the genomes of living organisms and is based on a simplified version of the bacterial CRISPR-Cas9 antiviral defense system [1].\n- Cas9 nuclease, delivered with a synthetic guide RNA (gRNA), cuts the genome at a desired location [1].\n- Genome editing inserts, deletes, modifies, or replaces DNA at site-specific locations, using programmable molecules that create double-strand breaks [3].\n- In prokaryotes, CRISPR sequences derive from bacteriophage DNA and are used to detect and destroy similar phages in later infections [2].\n- A Hacker News story titled \"CRISPR gene-editing breakthrough opens door to treating broad array of diseases\" links to NPR [4]. Another reports that HIV in cell culture can be eliminated using CRISPR-Cas gene editing [5]."}

